IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `ea-part2`
IMPORT `ea-part2-continuity`
IMPORT `ea-part3-salary`
IMPORT `ea-part4-hours`
IMPORT `ea-part9-maternity`
IMPORT `ea-part10-leave`
IMPORT `ea-part12-records`
IMPORT `ea-part12-retrenchment`
IMPORT `ea-retrenchment-reporting-notification`

-- Employment Act 1968 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, informal
-- consolidation in force from 5 December 2025 -- THE SEAT LAYER.
--
-- SEAT: the employee (one person, in one employment with one employer).
--
-- This layer phrases the rules already encoded in the modules imported above
-- as the questions an employee would ask about their own job: how much notice
-- they are owed, whether they can be dismissed or suspended, when they must be
-- paid, what may be deducted, what their rest days, overtime and leave are
-- worth, what maternity protection they have, and what their employer must
-- give them or report. Every question below calls an existing rule with the
-- same parameters and CHANGES NONE OF THEM; nothing here is new law.
--
-- Where the existing encoding departs from the defaults ruled on 2026-10-18
-- (recorded, not fixed, in this pass):
--   * Most rules are phrased from the employer's or the Commissioner's side
--     ("the employer has complied with section 95(1)", "section 38(1)(a) was
--     contravened", "employing this child contravenes section 68(1)"); this
--     layer re-phrases the main ones and leaves the rest as they are.
--   * Judgments the Act leaves to someone else are taken as plain facts rather
--     than answered conditionally: whether the employee had a "reasonable
--     excuse" (s 13(2)), whether the employer held a "due inquiry" and there was
--     misconduct (s 14(1)), whether danger is "immediate" (s 15), whether work is
--     "light work suited to the child's capacity" (s 68(3)).
--   * Enforcement machinery is encoded beside the operative rules: the s 96A
--     administrative penalties, contravention notices, reconsideration and
--     directions (ss 126A-126D), the secrecy provisions (ss 101-102), and the
--     s 82 offence.
--   * The instruments that set the Act's prescribed numbers (record retention,
--     pay slip timing, the Fifth Schedule, the s 95A minimum period) were not
--     retrieved, so those numbers are supplied facts; only the Retrenchment
--     Reporting Notification 2019 is brought in.
--   * Length of service under s 10(3) is asked band by band rather than
--     worked out from dates (see NOTES.md, observation 2).

§ `Employment Act 1968 -- the employee's questions`

§§ `Notice, and leaving the job`

-- ss 8, 10(2), 10(3). "Notice" is the warning either side must give to end
-- the contract of service. The contract's figure governs unless it is shorter
-- than the Act's minimum for the employee's length of service, in which case
-- s 8 voids it and the Act's minimum applies.
GIVEN `the terms` IS A ContractTerms, `the service` IS A ServiceLength
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How many days of notice am I owed` IS
    `the notice period that governs, in days` `the terms` `the service`

-- s 11(1). "Salary in lieu of notice": instead of notice, a party may end the
-- contract by paying the salary, at the gross rate of pay (pay before
-- deductions), that would have accrued over the notice period.
GIVEN `the gross daily rate of pay` IS A NUMBER
    , `the terms` IS A ContractTerms
    , `the service` IS A ServiceLength
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How much must I be paid in lieu of notice` IS
    `the payment in lieu of notice` `the gross daily rate of pay` `the terms` `the service`

-- s 13(2). "Deemed to have broken the contract": treated by the Act as having
-- ended the contract by breach, as if the employee had walked out. Absence of
-- more than 2 days continuously, without prior leave, and either no reasonable
-- excuse or no attempt to tell the employer of it.
GIVEN `the absence` IS AN Absence
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I treated as having broken my contract by being absent` IF
    `the employee is deemed to have broken the contract` `the absence`

-- s 15. An employee may end the contract without notice if they or their
-- dependant are threatened with immediate danger to the person by violence or
-- disease which they did not undertake to run by the contract of service.
GIVEN `the facts` IS A DangerFacts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I leave without notice because of danger` IF
    `the employee may terminate without notice under section 15` `the facts`

§§ `Dismissal and suspension`

-- s 14(1). "Due inquiry": a proper inquiry by the employer into the alleged
-- misconduct before acting on it. "Misconduct inconsistent with the conditions
-- of service": conduct that breaks the express or implied terms of the job.
GIVEN `the facts` IS A DismissalFacts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can my employer dismiss me without notice for misconduct` IF
    `the employer may dismiss without notice under section 14(1)` `the facts`

-- s 14(1)(b). Instead of dismissing, the employer may suspend without salary
-- for a period not exceeding one week, after the same due inquiry.
GIVEN `the facts` IS A DismissalFacts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my suspension without pay lawful` IF
    `the suspension is lawful under section 14(1)(b)` `the facts`

-- s 14(2A). Only a "relevant employee" may claim for wrongful dismissal: any
-- employee not in a managerial or executive position; a manager or executive
-- dismissed without notice and without salary in lieu; or a manager or
-- executive dismissed with notice (or salary in lieu) after 6 months' service.
GIVEN `the employee` IS A Dismissed
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I claim for wrongful dismissal` IF
    `the employee may lodge a wrongful dismissal claim` `the employee`

-- s 45. "Retrenchment benefit": a payment on dismissal for redundancy or
-- reorganisation. Under 2 years' continuous service, the Act says none is
-- owed.
GIVEN `the service` IS A ServiceForBenefits
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I barred from a retrenchment benefit by my short service` IF
    `section 45 bars any retrenchment benefit` `the service`

§§ `Being paid`

-- ss 21 to 23. Salary must be paid within 7 days after the salary period, 14
-- days for overtime, 3 days after a dismissal, on the day the contract ends
-- after due notice, and within 7 days where the employee leaves without notice.
GIVEN `the occasion` IS A PaymentOccasion, `the days taken to pay` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Was I paid late` IF
    `the payment was late` `the occasion` `the days taken to pay`

-- ss 27 and 32 with s 27(1)(d)-(e) and (i). An "authorised deduction" is one the
-- Act lists; some of them need the employee's written consent, which the
-- employee may withdraw in writing before the deduction is made.
GIVEN `the kind` IS A DeductionKind, `the consent` IS A Consent
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can my employer make this deduction from my salary` IF
    `the deduction may lawfully be made` `the kind` `the consent`

-- s 29. A deduction for damage or loss caused by the employee's neglect or
-- default: no more than the loss, no more than a quarter of a month's wages
-- unless the Commissioner (the Commissioner for Labour) permits more, and only
-- after the employee has had a chance to "show cause" (explain why it should
-- not be made).
GIVEN `the deduction` IS A DamagesDeduction, `the proportions` IS A Proportions
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can my employer deduct this amount for damage or loss I caused` IF
    `the damages deduction may be made` `the deduction` `the proportions`

§§ `Hours, rest days and overtime`

-- s 35. Part 4 (rest days, hours, overtime) covers a "workman" (an employee
-- doing mainly manual labour) earning up to $4,500 a month, and any other
-- employee earning up to $2,600 a month, but not managers or executives.
GIVEN `the worker` IS A Worker, `the caps` IS A SalaryCaps
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do the rest day, hours and overtime rules cover me` IF
    `this Part applies to the employee` `the worker` `the caps`

-- s 37. The pay for working on a "rest day" (the employee's one whole day off
-- a week), which doubles where the work was at the employer's request.
GIVEN `the work` IS A RestDayWork
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What must I be paid for working on my rest day` IS
    `the payment for working on the rest day` `the work`

-- s 38(4). Overtime is paid at not less than one and a half times the
-- "hourly basic rate of pay" (basic pay, without allowances, per hour).
GIVEN `the extra hours` IS A NUMBER, `the hourly basic rate of pay` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is my overtime pay` IS
    `the overtime payment` `the extra hours` `the hourly basic rate of pay`

§§ `Leave`

-- s 88A. "Annual leave": paid leave, 7 days in the first year of service, one
-- more day each year to 14; pro-rated in a part year; nothing under 3 months'
-- service, and lost entirely for absence without permission or reasonable
-- excuse on more than 20% of working days.
GIVEN `the service` IS A LeaveService
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How many days of annual leave do I get` IS
    `the annual leave days the employee is entitled to` `the service`

-- s 89. Paid sick leave after examination by a medical practitioner: up to 14
-- days of outpatient sick leave a year, or up to 60 where hospitalisation is
-- necessary, scaled down in the first 6 months of service.
GIVEN `the facts` IS A SickLeaveFacts
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How many days of paid sick leave can I take` IS
    `the paid sick leave ceiling in days` `the facts`

§§ `Maternity`

-- s 76(1A), (2B) and s 83. The "benefit period" is the 8 weeks of the
-- maternity absence that the employer must pay for; it needs 3 months' service
-- before confinement (giving birth), and is forfeited by working for another
-- employer during the absence.
GIVEN `the facts` IS A MaternityFacts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I entitled to be paid during my maternity benefit period` IF
    `she is entitled to payment during the benefit period` `the facts`

-- s 76(1A). The payment is the gross rate of pay for each day of the 8-week
-- benefit period.
GIVEN `the facts` IS A MaternityFacts
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How much maternity pay do I get` IS
    `the payment for the benefit period` `the facts`

-- s 81. While absent on maternity leave under Part 9, the employer may not
-- give notice of dismissal during the absence, or on a day such that the
-- notice would expire during it.
GIVEN `the employee is absent in accordance with this Part` IS A BOOLEAN
    , `notice of dismissal was given during the absence` IS A BOOLEAN
    , `the notice would expire during the absence` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is the notice of dismissal I was given during my maternity absence unlawful` IF
    `the notice of dismissal is unlawful under section 81` `the employee is absent in accordance with this Part` `notice of dismissal was given during the absence` `the notice would expire during the absence`

§§ `What my employer must give me or report`

-- s 95A. "Key employment terms": the written record of the main terms of the
-- job, which must be given within 14 days of starting (for contracts made on
-- or after 1 April 2016 and service of at least the prescribed minimum).
GIVEN `the facts` IS A KeyTermsFacts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Has my employer failed to give me my key employment terms in time` IF
    `the employer has contravened section 95A` `the facts`

-- s 96A and the Employment (Retrenchment Reporting) Notification 2019. Where
-- an employer with 10 or more covered employees gives a covered employee
-- notice of "retrenchment" (termination at the employer's initiative for
-- redundancy or reorganisation), it must report it to the Commissioner.
GIVEN `the notice` IS A `Notice of retrenchment`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must my employer report my retrenchment to the Commissioner` IF
    `a retrenchment report must be given for the notice` `the notice`
